1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,910
Zero watts… zero ice

2
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:03,592
yet this clay jug cools water!

3
00:00:03,592 --> 00:00:04,034
How?

4
00:00:04,234 --> 00:00:05,693
Clay is full of tiny pores

5
00:00:05,693 --> 00:00:07,005
Water slowly seeps through them

6
00:00:07,005 --> 00:00:08,488
out onto the jug's surface

7
00:00:08,688 --> 00:00:10,249
Hot, dry air evaporates it

8
00:00:10,249 --> 00:00:12,328
and evaporation needs energy: heat

9
00:00:12,328 --> 00:00:14,162
It takes that heat from the water inside

10
00:00:14,162 --> 00:00:16,174
so the water cools, with no power

11
00:00:16,374 --> 00:00:17,248
The drier the air

12
00:00:17,248 --> 00:00:19,287
the more it evaporates, the more it cools

13
00:00:19,287 --> 00:00:21,320
In humid air? It barely works

14
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:23,026
Your body uses the same trick

15
00:00:23,026 --> 00:00:24,284
when you sweat to cool down

16
00:00:24,484 --> 00:00:26,474
So the clay jug sweats, just like you

17
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:27,621
to keep your water cold!
